Z||000006||rogergarrison||11-10-2001||04:06 AM||||I was at a big car show near Wooster, Ohio few years back and there was a black 88 with a V6, and I think auto, that had a turbo. He had a bunch of articles and ads displayed along with the original window sticker showing it. Maybe it was done as a dealer add on. But he said it was one of a very few that were factory built, and he bought it new off the showroom floor just like it was. Not saying it was true, but he did have the stuff to back him up, so just FYI.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||11-10-2001||01:39 PM||||Pontiac never mass produced turbo Fieros. It wasn't even a "secret" option. If it had been ALL of us would have known about it. The Redshift 2.2 turbo was a dealer option through only 2 dealers in the world including Phil Huff of FOCOA. Including the prototype, I think there were 11 of them. Though I have never seen one or even heard of anyone tucking one away for safe keeping. Fieros with V6s at the time were being penalized with high insurance rates. One could just imagine trying to insure a factory turbo V6 GT.<P>Pontiac had been messing around with prototype turbo intercooled all aluminum 2.9s (according to Witzenberg's "Fiero") that ran between 220-235 hp with 0-60 in 6.2 seconds. I doubt it had gone out "the back door."<P>Unless the guy has the documentation to back it up I would have to wonder if this guy wouldn't eventually say, "Well, thats what I was told..."||||reg||2
Z||000008||Formula88||11-10-2001||03:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WKDFIRO:<BR><B>Pontiac had been messing around with prototype turbo intercooled all aluminum 2.9s (according to Witzenberg's "Fiero") that ran between 220-235 hp with 0-60 in 6.2 seconds.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I remember reading about the 2.9 turbo Fiero.  That was supposed to be out for 89 or 90.  That's supposedly one of the big reasons for the Fiero's demise (if you believe the Vette conspiracy theory).  The 2.9 turbo Fiero would have chewed up the 89 or 90 Vettes, and Chevy couldn't let that happen.  The Fiero would have been faster, better handling, and cheaper.  Whoops! <P>I don't know if Chevy had a hand in the Fiero's demise or not.  But I believe that they did.  It wouldn't be the first time.  Chevy kept Pontiac from getting a 2 seater in the 60's, and that's why we got the Firebird instead.||||reg||1
